\subsection{Centrality as a Potential Confound on Homogeneous Schemas}
\label{sec:centrality_confound}

Per-type analysis on MAVEN reveals an anomalous \emph{positive} BCS--F1 correlation ($\rho = +0.184\text{--}0.208$, $p < 0.03$): FrameNet-derived types cluster tightly \citep{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}, conflating confusability with centrality. Controlling for log frequency and degree centrality, the correlation vanishes ($p > 0.2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}), though the pairwise SCS signal remains intact ($r = 0.129$, $p < 0.001$). Few-NERD's cross-domain schema shows the expected negative direction ($\rho = -0.293$, $p = 0.02$); mean BCS is reliable only for cross-domain schemas.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless otherwise noted, all experiments in this section use the v1 prompt (\S\ref{sec:model}; full templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}).

\subsection{Count-Degradation Curves}
\label{sec:count_curves}

Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a shows RE accuracy declines monotonically with schema size (0.86$\to$0.50 for FewRel). NER recall declines through intermediate counts (0.51$\to$0.27, plateauing across counts 22--44) before partial recovery at full-schema (0.41), where the elimination of type-filtering overhead restores extraction performance. DocRED replicates the monotonic RE pattern (88.0\%$\to$38.7\%, $-$56\%; Appendix). MAVEN exhibits a reversed trend attributable to centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). The effect replicates on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct ($-$43\,pp on Few-NERD; per-batch $r = 0.725$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}). The pattern is stable across three seeds (max $\Delta = 10.7$\,pp, within 1\,SD; intra-batch variance exceeds inter-seed variance by ${>}$5$\times$; Appendix~\ref{app:variance-decomposition}). Formal trend analysis confirms monotonic RE decline (Jonckheere-Terpstra $J = 109{,}707$, $p < 0.001$). Count curves establish trend direction; magnitude estimation relies on the higher-powered BCS-controlled experiments (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\subsection{Pairwise Confusability and Type Confusion}
\label{sec:pairwise}

We test whether pairwise SCS predicts type-level confusion rates. For each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the confusion rate (the fraction of $t_i$ gold instances misclassified as $t_j$) and correlate it with $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)$. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports Pearson correlations with bootstrap 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Embed.} & \textbf{$n$ pairs} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$r^2$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & Qwen3 & 1{,}953 & 0.112 & 0.013 & {[}0.076, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
MAVEN & MiniLM & 11{,}026 & 0.129 & 0.017 & {[}0.104, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Pairwise SCS--confusion correlation. Confusion rate is bidirectional $C(t_i{\to}t_j) + C(t_j{\to}t_i)$ divided by gold frequency sum; CIs are percentile bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$). FewRel ($r = 0.187$; \S\ref{sec:scs-validation}) excluded (per-instance RE format). Type-level cluster bootstrap CIs exclude zero (Few-NERD [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN [0.081, 0.185]).}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

